    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> had come under the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in 1720, after the Qing expelled the forces of the Dzungar Khanate, but by the end of the 19th century Qing authority there had become little more than ritual and symbol. In 1910, responding to British encroachment, the Qing sent an expedition to assert greater control. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a> of 1911–1912 Tibetan militia attacked the Qing garrison, and the Qing officials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> were forced to sign the Three Point Agreement for the surrender and expulsion of their forces from central Tibet.</p><p>The new Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> claimed all the Qing lands, Tibet among them, on the strength of the abdication edict signed by the Empress Dowager Longyu, and its provisional constitution named Tibet an integral part of the state. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> offered to restore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubten-gyatso">13th Dalai Lama</a>'s titles; he refused, replying that he intended to exercise both temporal and ecclesiastical rule. Returning to Lhasa in 1913, he proclaimed that the relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> had been that of patron and priest. In January 1913 Agvan Dorzhiev signed a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Urga</a> proclaiming mutual recognition.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Traditional Tibetan society had a feudal class structure. In the words of Donald S. Lopez, power was monopolised by an elite of a small aristocracy, the hierarchs of the various sects and the great Geluk monasteries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubten-gyatso">13th Dalai Lama</a> had reformed the serf system in the first decade of the 20th century, and by 1950 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> had probably ceased to exist in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, though it persisted in places such as the Chumbi Valley, and beggars, the ragyabas, were endemic.</p><p>Land was held in hereditary estates, or shiga, granted by the state and revocable, made up of demesne land held by nobles or monasteries and village land held by the central government. Outside the nobility and the monasteries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> were classed as serfs of two kinds: the düchung, bound to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> labour on estates but holding their own plots and free to move outside their labour periods, and the village tralpa, bound for taxes and transport duties. Half of the village serfs had bought their freedom. A serf absent from his estate for three years became a commoner. The government issued its own banknotes and coins, and from 1912 ran its own postal service.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubten-gyatso">13th Dalai Lama</a> had taken full control in the 1910s, he built up the Tibetan Army with British advisers and weapons, meaning it to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and to bring in the Tibetan-inhabited lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a>. It grew to about 10,000 soldiers by 1936, adequately armed and trained infantry, but it almost entirely lacked machine guns, artillery, aircraft and tanks. Great numbers of poorly armed village militias served beside it. Often outgunned, it fought relatively well against Chinese warlords in the 1920s and 1930s.</p><p>In 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> regained Chamdo and western Kham. In 1932 Tibet invaded Qinghai after a dispute over a monastery in Yushu, and the Qinghai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> armies of Ma Bufang and Liu Wenhui defeated it in the Sino-Tibetan War; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> cabled the British for help and demoted generals who had surrendered. The 1934 Khamba Rebellion led by the Pandatsang family turned Khamba tribesmen against the Tibetan Army. Tibetan troops fought Kazakh refugees entering the plateau in the 1930s and killed the CIA agent Douglas Mackiernan on 29 April 1950. Against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> in 1950 the army disintegrated and surrendered without much resistance.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> was governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a>, the government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashag">Kashag</a> as its council of ministers. It was divided into eight chikyaps, each subdivided into dzongs and estates: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, administering 29 dzongs, Changgyu with its seat at Nagqu, Lhoka at Tsetang, Dakong, Ngari at Gar, Chomo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a>, and Domed or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> with its seat at Chamdo. Its government controlled Ü-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> and Kham west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a>, roughly the area of the present Tibet Autonomous Region.</p><p>Succession followed the line of incarnations. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubten-gyatso">13th Dalai Lama</a> died in December 1933, Lhamo Dhondup, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amdo">Amdo</a> in 1935, was recognised as his incarnation. On 26 January 1940 the Regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reting-rinpoche">Reting Rinpoche</a> asked the Chinese central government to exempt him from the lot-drawing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-urn">Golden Urn</a>, which it approved, and after a ransom of 400,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> dragons was paid to Ma Bufang he travelled to Lhasa and was enthroned at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a>. Chinese reports claimed that Wu Zhongxin presided at the ceremony, while the British representative Sir Basil Gould wrote that he was a passive spectator; the point remains disputed. Tibet set up a Foreign Office in 1942.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>'s status was the subject of rivalry between the British and Russian empires and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and no agreement settled it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simla-convention">Simla Convention</a> proposed an Outer Tibet, autonomous under Chinese suzerainty, and an Inner Tibet of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amdo">Amdo</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> rejected it, but the convention drew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mcmahon-line">McMahon Line</a>, which placed the Tawang tract, administered indirectly through the Tawang monastery, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In late 1947 the Tibetan government laid claim to districts south of that line in a note to independent India.</p><p>Tibet acted abroad as a state. In 1946 it sent missions to China and India, the first carrying a letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> that promised to maintain Tibet's independence, and in 1947 a delegation attended the Asian Relations Conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>, perhaps the first public appearance of the Tibetan national flag. In 1947–49 a trade mission led by Finance Minister Tsepon W. D. Shakabpa visited India, China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom, meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a> in 1948, though the countries it visited avoided supporting Tibetan independence. The Austrian Heinrich Harrer, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> from 1944, tutored the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> never renounced its claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and asserted it at intervals. After 1933 it opened an office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> under the Mongolian and Tibetan Affairs Commission, and the British opened one of their own. In 1942 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> had Ma Bufang repair the Yushu airport, move several thousand troops to the border and threatened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> with bombing. The same year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> told Chiang's government that it had never disputed Chinese claims to Tibet.</p><p>In 1949, seeing the Communists winning the civil war, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashag">Kashag</a> expelled all Chinese officials from Tibet. On 1 October 1949 the 10th Panchen Lama sent a telegram to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> welcoming the coming liberation of Tibet, and the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> soon moved to reassert Chinese presence. In June 1950 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> said it would recognise Chinese suzerainty only if Tibet were regarded as autonomous. In October 1950 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> entered Chamdo, defeating sporadic resistance. In 1951 a delegation headed by Ngapoi Ngawang Jigme, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>'s authorisation, negotiated in Beijing the Seventeen Point Agreement affirming China's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>; it was ratified in Lhasa months later, and China called it the "peaceful liberation of Tibet".</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Between the fall of the Qing in 1912 and the Seventeen Point Agreement of 1951, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> under the 13th and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lamas</a> governed itself, kept its own army, currency, post and foreign office, and called itself independent, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> maintained its claim and almost no state recognised Tibetan independence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a>'s advance in 1950 ended it.</em></p>

